---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../styles/global.css';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import RockTable from '../components/RockTable.astro';
---

<Base title="Stratum vs Alternatives: Multi-Tenancy Comparison | Node.js" description="Compare Stratum to rolling your own tenant_id, WorkOS, Frontegg, ABP.IO, Clerk / Auth0 Organizations, Nile, and Oso / Permit.io. See why Stratum is the multi-tenancy library Node.js has been missing.">

<Nav />

<!-- Page header -->
<main class="cmp-main">

  <header class="cmp-header cmp-header--compact">
    <h1>Stratum vs the <span class="accent">alternatives</span></h1>
    <p class="cmp-subtitle">
      Six honest comparisons, from rolling your own to auth-layer orgs,
      tenant databases, and authorization engines. Where each fits, and where Stratum does.
    </p>
  </header>

  <!-- Comparison 1: Rolling Your Own -->
  <section class="cmp-section">
    <div class="cmp-section-header">
      <h2>Stratum vs rolling your own (<code>tenant_id</code>)</h2>
      <p class="cmp-section-intro">
        The default choice. Add a column, filter every query, ship it. Works fine early on.
        The problem is what happens next.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="cmp-narrative">
      <p>
        On day one, rolling your own looks correct. A <code>tenant_id</code> column is simple,
        understood by every developer on your team, and requires no dependencies. You're not
        over-engineering. Good instinct.
      </p>
      <p>
        The issue isn't day one. It's month 18.
      </p>
      <p>
        By month 18, your hand-rolled solution has accumulated: a config table with no inheritance
        model, audit logging added retroactively and missing half the mutation points, no isolation
        options beyond the shared schema you started with, and GDPR compliance that lives in a
        Notion doc but not in code. When an enterprise customer demands a separate database for their
        data, you build that isolation yourself. Stratum sets the isolation strategy per tenant when
        you create the tenant, so that customer can start on a database of its own.
      </p>
      <p>
        Stratum gives you month-18 features on day one. Not because you need them all immediately,
        but because the architecture is already in place when you do.
      </p>
    </div>

    <RockTable
      label="Stratum vs rolling your own"
      columns={["Capability", "Rolling your own", "Stratum"]}
      rows={[
        { feat: "Setup time", cells: [["neutral", "1-2 days"], ["good", "5 minutes"]] },
        { feat: "Tenant hierarchy", cells: [["bad", "Build it yourself (weeks)"], ["good", "Built-in (ltree)"]] },
        { feat: "Config inheritance", cells: [["bad", "Build it yourself"], ["good", "Built-in, root-to-leaf"]] },
        { feat: "Isolation strategies", cells: [["neutral", "Manual RLS only"], ["good", "RLS, schema, database · per tenant"]] },
        { feat: "Audit logging", cells: [["bad", "Bolt on later"], ["good", "Every mutation, actor-attributed"]] },
        { feat: "GDPR (Art. 17 + 20)", cells: [["bad", "Build it yourself"], ["good", "Export + purge, built-in"]] },
        { feat: "Field encryption", cells: [["bad", "Build it yourself"], ["good", "AES-256-GCM, transparent"]] },
        { feat: "Month-18 cost", cells: [["bad", "3+ months engineering"], ["good", "Already done"]] },
      ]}
    />
  </section>

  <!-- Comparison 2: WorkOS / Frontegg -->
  <section class="cmp-section">
    <div class="cmp-section-header">
      <h2>Stratum vs WorkOS / Frontegg</h2>
      <p class="cmp-section-intro">
        WorkOS and Frontegg are excellent products. They solve a real problem. Just not the
        same problem Stratum solves.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="cmp-narrative">
      <p>
        WorkOS and Frontegg operate at the <strong>identity and authentication layer</strong>.
        They handle SSO, SCIM provisioning, audit logs for auth events, and organization
        management in the context of who can log in. They do this extremely well.
      </p>
      <p>
        Stratum operates at the <strong>data tenancy layer</strong>. It handles how your
        database stores, isolates, and manages data across tenants. These are different problems
        at different levels of your stack, and the right answer for most teams is both.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use WorkOS or Frontegg to manage authentication and user provisioning.
        Use Stratum to manage data isolation, config inheritance, and compliance operations
        on the PostgreSQL side. They complement each other. They don't compete.
      </p>
    </div>

    <RockTable
      label="Stratum vs WorkOS / Frontegg"
      columns={["Capability", "WorkOS / Frontegg", "Stratum"]}
      rows={[
        { feat: "SSO / SAML / SCIM", cells: [["good", "Yes, core product"], ["bad", "Not in scope"]] },
        { feat: "Auth event audit log", cells: [["good", "Yes"], ["bad", "Not in scope"]] },
        { feat: "Data isolation strategies", cells: [["bad", "No"], ["good", "RLS, schema, database"]] },
        { feat: "Tenant hierarchy / tree", cells: [["neutral", "Flat organizations only"], ["good", "Arbitrary depth, ltree-backed"]] },
        { feat: "Config inheritance", cells: [["bad", "No"], ["good", "Root-to-leaf, lockable keys"]] },
        { feat: "GDPR data operations", cells: [["neutral", "Partial (auth data only)"], ["good", "Full export + hard purge"]] },
        { feat: "Data mutation audit log", cells: [["bad", "No"], ["good", "Every mutation, actor-attributed"]] },
        { feat: "Pricing model", cells: [["neutral", "Proprietary SaaS"], ["good", "Open source, MIT"]] },
      ]}
    />

    <div class="cmp-callout layer">
      <p>
        <strong>The right stack:</strong> WorkOS or Frontegg for authentication and user
        management. Stratum for data tenancy and compliance. Both in the same application,
        each doing what it's built for.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Comparison 3: ABP.IO -->
  <section class="cmp-section">
    <div class="cmp-section-header">
      <h2>Stratum vs ABP Framework</h2>
      <p class="cmp-section-intro">
        ABP is the gold standard for multi-tenancy in the .NET ecosystem. Stratum is the
        equivalent for Node.js, the library that should have existed years ago.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="cmp-narrative">
      <p>
        ABP Framework (formerly ABP.IO) is a mature, opinionated application framework for
        .NET that includes multi-tenancy as a first-class concern. Teams building C# applications
        have had this foundation for years. It's well-documented, battle-tested, and handles
        the full lifecycle: tenant resolution, data isolation, config, and more.
      </p>
      <p>
        Node.js and TypeScript developers have had no equivalent. When we looked at what ABP
        provides and compared it to the npm ecosystem, the gap was stark. We built Stratum to
        close it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Stratum is not a port of ABP. The Node.js and TypeScript ecosystem has different
        conventions and constraints. But the motivation is the same: give developers in this
        ecosystem a stable, production-ready multi-tenancy foundation so they stop reinventing it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <RockTable
      label="Stratum vs ABP Framework"
      columns={["Capability", "ABP Framework (.NET)", "Stratum (Node.js)"]}
      rows={[
        { feat: "Runtime", cells: [["neutral", ".NET / C#"], ["good", "Node.js / TypeScript"]] },
        { feat: "Tenant resolution", cells: [["good", "Yes (header, subdomain, JWT)"], ["good", "Yes (SDK middleware)"]] },
        { feat: "Data isolation", cells: [["good", "Schema + database strategies"], ["good", "RLS, schema, database"]] },
        { feat: "Tenant hierarchy", cells: [["neutral", "Parent-child (2 levels typical)"], ["good", "Arbitrary depth, ltree-backed"]] },
        { feat: "Config inheritance", cells: [["good", "Settings system"], ["good", "Root-to-leaf, lockable keys"]] },
        { feat: "GDPR tooling", cells: [["neutral", "Partial via modules"], ["good", "Export + purge, built-in"]] },
        { feat: "Open source", cells: [["neutral", "Partial (community edition)"], ["good", "Fully open source, MIT"]] },
        { feat: "npm / TypeScript native", cells: [["bad", "No"], ["good", "Yes, built for the ecosystem"]] },
      ]}
    />
  </section>

  <!-- Comparison 4: Clerk / Auth0 Organizations -->
  <section class="cmp-section">
    <div class="cmp-section-header">
      <h2>Stratum vs Clerk Organizations</h2>
      <p class="cmp-section-intro">
        Clerk Organizations (and Auth0 Organizations) give you multi-tenant <em>auth</em>:
        org membership, invitations, and SSO scoped per organization. Stratum gives you
        multi-tenant <em>data</em>. Different halves of the same feature.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="cmp-narrative">
      <p>
        Clerk Organizations and Auth0 Organizations live at the <strong>authentication and
        membership layer</strong>. They model who belongs to which org, hand you a polished
        invitation and member-management UI, let each organization bring its own SSO connection,
        and put an <code>org_id</code> in the session token. For the login and org-switching
        experience, they are hard to beat.
      </p>
      <p>
        What they deliberately don't touch is the layer below the token. They don't isolate a
        tenant's rows in your database, inherit config down through nested tenants, enforce
        row-level security, audit the data mutations your app performs, or purge a tenant's data
        for a GDPR request. That's not a gap in the products. It's simply not the layer they
        operate at.
      </p>
      <p>
        Stratum takes the <code>org_id</code> those systems already resolve and builds the tenant
        data layer beneath it: the isolation strategy, the config tree, permission delegation, and
        the compliance workflows. The two compose cleanly: Clerk decides who is in the org,
        Stratum decides what that org's data can do and where it lives.
      </p>
    </div>

    <RockTable
      label="Stratum vs Clerk / Auth0 Organizations"
      columns={["Capability", "Clerk / Auth0 Orgs", "Stratum"]}
      rows={[
        { feat: "Org membership + invites", cells: [["good", "Yes, core product"], ["bad", "Not in scope"]] },
        { feat: "SSO per organization", cells: [["good", "Yes"], ["bad", "Not in scope"]] },
        { feat: "Data isolation strategies", cells: [["bad", "No"], ["good", "RLS, schema, database"]] },
        { feat: "Nested tenants", cells: [["neutral", "Flat orgs (+ optional sub-orgs)"], ["good", "Arbitrary depth, ltree-backed"]] },
        { feat: "Config inheritance", cells: [["bad", "No"], ["good", "Root-to-leaf, lockable keys"]] },
        { feat: "Data mutation audit log", cells: [["bad", "No"], ["good", "Every mutation, before/after state"]] },
        { feat: "GDPR purge of tenant data", cells: [["bad", "No"], ["good", "Full export + hard purge"]] },
        { feat: "Pricing model", cells: [["neutral", "Proprietary SaaS"], ["good", "Open source, MIT"]] },
      ]}
    />

    <div class="cmp-callout layer">
      <p>
        <strong>Use both:</strong> Clerk (or Auth0) for authentication and the org-membership UI
        your users see. Stratum for the tenant data layer beneath it: isolation, config, and
        compliance. They sit at different layers and slot together.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Comparison 5: Nile -->
  <section class="cmp-section">
    <div class="cmp-section-header">
      <h2>Stratum vs Nile</h2>
      <p class="cmp-section-intro">
        Nile (thenile.dev) is a tenant-aware Postgres you adopt and host with them. Stratum is a
        library over the Postgres you <em>already</em> run. The split is: their database, or your
        database with a tenancy model on top.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="cmp-narrative">
      <p>
        Nile virtualizes tenants at the <strong>database</strong> itself. You build on Nile's
        hosted Postgres and get per-tenant isolation baked into the platform. If you're greenfield
        and comfortable putting your data on a startup database vendor, it's an elegant story:
        tenancy is a property of the database, not something you assemble in application code.
      </p>
      <p>
        Stratum takes the opposite stance on <em>where</em> tenancy lives. It's an MIT-licensed
        library that runs over your existing PostgreSQL: RDS, Supabase, Neon, or self-hosted.
        So there's no database to migrate to and no vendor to depend on. On top of that Postgres it
        adds the application-layer tenancy model Nile doesn't aim to cover: nested tenant
        hierarchy, config inheritance with lockable keys, permission delegation, and GDPR
        export/purge workflows.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the choice is less feature-by-feature and more about ownership. Nile asks you to adopt a
        database; Stratum asks you to keep yours and drop in a layer. If nested tenancy, config
        inheritance, or compliance operations matter to you, those are Stratum's territory
        regardless of which Postgres you run.
      </p>
    </div>

    <RockTable
      label="Stratum vs Nile"
      columns={["Capability", "Nile", "Stratum"]}
      rows={[
        { feat: "What it is", cells: [["neutral", "Hosted tenant-aware Postgres"], ["good", "Library over your Postgres"]] },
        { feat: "Runs on existing DB", cells: [["bad", "Adopt their database"], ["good", "RDS, Supabase, Neon, self-hosted"]] },
        { feat: "Per-tenant isolation", cells: [["good", "Yes, at the DB"], ["good", "RLS, schema, database"]] },
        { feat: "Nested tenant hierarchy", cells: [["neutral", "Flat tenants"], ["good", "Arbitrary depth, ltree-backed"]] },
        { feat: "Config inheritance", cells: [["bad", "No"], ["good", "Root-to-leaf, lockable keys"]] },
        { feat: "Permission delegation / ABAC", cells: [["bad", "No"], ["good", "Delegation modes + ABAC policies"]] },
        { feat: "GDPR export + purge", cells: [["bad", "No"], ["good", "Article 20 export, Article 17 purge"]] },
        { feat: "Lock-in / licensing", cells: [["neutral", "Vendor-hosted"], ["good", "No lock-in, MIT"]] },
      ]}
    />

    <div class="cmp-callout layer">
      <p>
        <strong>Choose by ownership:</strong> greenfield and happy to host your data with a startup
        database vendor → Nile is a genuinely elegant fit. Existing Postgres, or you need nested
        tenancy, config inheritance, or compliance workflows → Stratum layers onto what you already
        run.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Comparison 6: Oso / Permit.io -->
  <section class="cmp-section">
    <div class="cmp-section-header">
      <h2>Stratum vs Oso / Permit.io</h2>
      <p class="cmp-section-intro">
        Oso and Permit.io are authorization engines: policy-as-code for evaluating who can do what.
        Stratum is a tenancy layer that happens to include scoped permissions. They overlap at one
        edge and diverge everywhere else.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="cmp-narrative">
      <p>
        Oso and Permit.io are built to answer one question extremely well: given a subject, an
        action, and a resource, is it allowed? They give you a policy language, ReBAC/ABAC
        evaluation, and a place to centralize app-wide authorization logic. If your access rules are
        rich and cross-cutting, a dedicated policy engine is the right tool.
      </p>
      <p>
        What they don't provide is the tenancy substrate: the tenant data model, the isolation
        strategies (RLS, schema, database), config inheritance down a tenant tree, tenant lifecycle
        operations like onboarding, archive, and purge, or an audit trail of tenant data mutations.
        A policy engine decides <em>whether</em> an action is allowed; it doesn't store, isolate, or
        manage the tenants themselves.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is one honest overlap. Stratum's permission delegation and ABAC cover permissions
        <em>within a tenant hierarchy</em>: delegation modes with cascade revocation, and ABAC
        policies that inherit down the tree. That's narrower and more opinionated than a general
        policy engine, and for tenancy-scoped access it's usually enough on its own.
      </p>
    </div>

    <RockTable
      label="Stratum vs Oso / Permit.io"
      columns={["Capability", "Oso / Permit.io", "Stratum"]}
      rows={[
        { feat: "General policy-as-code authz", cells: [["good", "Yes, core product"], ["neutral", "Tenancy-scoped only"]] },
        { feat: "ReBAC / ABAC evaluation", cells: [["good", "Yes, general-purpose"], ["good", "ABAC + hierarchical inheritance"]] },
        { feat: "Tenant data model", cells: [["bad", "No"], ["good", "Hierarchy, ltree-backed"]] },
        { feat: "Isolation strategies", cells: [["bad", "No"], ["good", "RLS, schema, database"]] },
        { feat: "Config inheritance", cells: [["bad", "No"], ["good", "Root-to-leaf, lockable keys"]] },
        { feat: "Tenant lifecycle", cells: [["bad", "No"], ["good", "Onboard, archive, purge"]] },
        { feat: "Data mutation audit log", cells: [["bad", "No"], ["good", "Every mutation, before/after state"]] },
        { feat: "Licensing", cells: [["neutral", "Open core / SaaS"], ["good", "Open source, MIT"]] },
      ]}
    />

    <div class="cmp-callout layer">
      <p>
        <strong>Choose by scope:</strong> complex, app-wide authorization policy → pair an authz
        engine like Oso or Permit.io with Stratum's tenant layer. Permissions that only ever run
        within your tenant hierarchy → Stratum's built-in delegation and ABAC are usually enough on
        their own.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Summary -->
  <section class="cmp-summary">
    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <ul class="rock-rows cmp-summary-rows" role="list">
      <li class="rock-row cmp-summary-row">
        <span class="cmp-summary-label">vs tenant_id</span>
        <p>The DIY approach works until it doesn't. Stratum gives you the infrastructure you'll need at month 18, from day one.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="rock-row cmp-summary-row">
        <span class="cmp-summary-label">vs WorkOS / Frontegg</span>
        <p>Different layer of the stack. Use them for auth. Use Stratum for data. They complement each other.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="rock-row cmp-summary-row">
        <span class="cmp-summary-label">vs ABP Framework</span>
        <p>ABP is great for .NET. Stratum fills the same role for Node.js. If you're writing TypeScript, there's no equivalent, until now.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="rock-row cmp-summary-row">
        <span class="cmp-summary-label">vs Clerk / Auth0 Orgs</span>
        <p>They own auth and org membership. Stratum owns the tenant data layer beneath the token: isolation, config, compliance. Compose them.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="rock-row cmp-summary-row">
        <span class="cmp-summary-label">vs Nile</span>
        <p>Nile is a database you host with them. Stratum is a library over the Postgres you already run, plus nested tenancy and compliance. No lock-in.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="rock-row cmp-summary-row">
        <span class="cmp-summary-label">vs Oso / Permit.io</span>
        <p>Policy engines evaluate authz; they don't model tenants. Pair one with Stratum for app-wide policy, or use Stratum alone for tenancy-scoped permissions.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- ORM Comparisons -->
  <section class="cmp-orm-links">
    <h2>See how Stratum works with your stack</h2>
    <p class="cmp-orm-intro">Stratum integrates with the ORM and database you already use. No migration required.</p>
    <div class="cmp-orm-grid">
      <a href="/compare/prisma/" class="cmp-orm-item">
        <span class="cmp-orm-name">Prisma</span>
        <span class="cmp-orm-arrow">&rarr;</span>
      </a>
      <a href="/compare/drizzle/" class="cmp-orm-item">
        <span class="cmp-orm-name">Drizzle</span>
        <span class="cmp-orm-arrow">&rarr;</span>
      </a>
      <a href="/compare/sequelize/" class="cmp-orm-item">
        <span class="cmp-orm-name">Sequelize</span>
        <span class="cmp-orm-arrow">&rarr;</span>
      </a>
      <a href="/compare/knex/" class="cmp-orm-item">
        <span class="cmp-orm-name">Knex</span>
        <span class="cmp-orm-arrow">&rarr;</span>
      </a>
      <a href="/compare/mongodb/" class="cmp-orm-item">
        <span class="cmp-orm-name">MongoDB</span>
        <span class="cmp-orm-arrow">&rarr;</span>
      </a>
      <a href="/compare/mysql/" class="cmp-orm-item">
        <span class="cmp-orm-name">MySQL</span>
        <span class="cmp-orm-arrow">&rarr;</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA -->
  <section class="cmp-cta layer">
    <h2>Start with Stratum today</h2>
    <div class="cmp-install">
      <code>npm install <span class="no-break">@stratum-hq/lib</span> pg</code>
    </div>
    <div class="cmp-cta-links">
      <a href="https://docs.stratum-hq.org/getting-started/quick-start/" class="btn-primary">Get Started</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/stratum-hq/Stratum" class="btn-secondary">GitHub</a>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>

<Footer />

<style>
  .cmp-main {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: calc(var(--space-8) * 3) var(--space-6) var(--space-16);
  }

  /* Header: compact, data-forward */
  .cmp-header {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-12);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .cmp-header--compact {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-8);
    text-align: left;
  }

  .cmp-header h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.25rem, 5vw, 2.75rem);
    font-weight: 900;
    line-height: 0.95;
    color: var(--text-primary);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }

  .cmp-subtitle {
    font-size: 1rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.6;
    max-width: 560px;
  }

  /* Sections */
  .cmp-section {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-16);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-top: var(--space-8);
  }

  .cmp-section-header {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }

  .cmp-section-header h2 {
    font-size: 1.875rem;
    font-weight: 900;
    color: var(--text-primary);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
    line-height: 0.95;
  }

  .cmp-section-header code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
    color: var(--code-text);
    background: var(--code-bg);
    padding: 2px 6px;
  }

  /* Prose holds a 65ch measure. The 800px column is for the tables. */
  .cmp-section-intro {
    max-width: 65ch;
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  /* Narrative prose */
  .cmp-narrative {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }

  .cmp-narrative p {
    max-width: 65ch;
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .cmp-narrative p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .cmp-narrative strong {
    color: var(--text-primary);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .cmp-narrative code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.875em;
    color: var(--code-text);
    background: var(--code-bg);
    padding: 2px 6px;
  }

  /* Callout: a ledge panel (.layer in global.css). The top padding clears
     the 10px rock lip. */
  .cmp-callout {
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
    padding: calc(var(--space-4) + 10px) var(--space-6) var(--space-4);
  }

  .cmp-callout p {
    max-width: 65ch;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  .cmp-callout strong {
    color: var(--text-primary);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  /* Summary */
  .cmp-summary {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-12);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-top: var(--space-8);
  }

  .cmp-summary h2 {
    font-size: 1.875rem;
    font-weight: 900;
    color: var(--text-primary);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
    line-height: 0.95;
  }

  /* The summary is one stack of rock rows: the comparison, then the verdict. */
  .cmp-summary-rows {
    list-style: none;
  }

  .cmp-summary-row {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 12rem) minmax(0, 1fr);
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  .cmp-summary-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text-primary);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
  }

  .cmp-summary-row p {
    max-width: 65ch;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .cmp-summary-row {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }

  /* CTA */
  /* A ledge panel (.layer in global.css). The top padding clears the lip. */
  .cmp-cta {
